Nginx on aapanel blocks PHP session cookies on cross-domain POST requests, causing Laravel 419 error
<h2>Hello,</h2>
<p>I'm facing a persistent <code>session/CSRF</code> issue with my Laravel SPA setup on aapanel, and I've concluded the problem lies within the server configuration.</p>
<p><strong>Setup:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Frontend (React):</strong> <a href="https://andiamo.elenmorcreative.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://andiamo.elenmorcreative.com</a></li>
<li><strong>Backend (Laravel 12):</strong> <a href="https://andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com</a></li>
<li><strong>Server:</strong> Nginx on aapanel</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The Problem:</strong>
My frontend fails to log in, always receiving a <code>419 CSRF token mismatch</code> error from Laravel. We have debugged this extensively and confirmed the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>A <code>GET</code> request to <code>/sanctum/csrf-cookie</code> is <strong>successful (204 No Content)</strong>, and the browser correctly receives and stores the <code>XSRF-TOKEN</code> and <code>laravel_session</code> cookies.</li>
<li>The React frontend correctly reads the <code>XSRF-TOKEN</code> cookie and sends it back in the <code>X-XSRF-TOKEN</code> header on the <code>POST /login</code> request.</li>
<li>The <code>POST /login</code> request is always rejected with a <code>419</code> error, indicating the server-side session is not being persisted between the <code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code> requests</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Proof that the issue is the server environment (not Laravel):</strong>
To isolate the problem, I created a simple <code>test.php</code> file in the <code>public</code> directory to test session persistence without the Laravel framework.</p>
<p><code>test.php</code> content:</p>
<pre><code><?php
header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://andiamo.elenmorcreative.com");
header("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true");
session_start();

if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
    echo "Session data on POST: " . ($_SESSION['test'] ?? 'NOT FOUND');
} else {
    $_SESSION['test'] = 'Hello World';
    echo "Session data on GET: " . $_SESSION['test'];
}
</code></pre>
<p>When I test this script (first a <code>GET</code>, then a <code>POST</code>) from my frontend's browser console, the session data is "NOT FOUND" on the <code>POST</code> request. This proves that even a basic PHP session is not being maintained across requests. The problem is not with Laravel, but with how Nginx/PHP-FPM is handling sessions.</p>
<p><strong>My Current Nginx Configuration:</strong>
Here is the full and current Nginx configuration for my backend site (<code>andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com</code>). I have already tried simplifying it to a standard Laravel setup.</p>
<pre><code>server {
    listen 80;
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com;
    root /www/wwwroot/andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com/andiamo-backend/public;

    # SSL Config
    ssl_certificate /www/server/panel/vhost/cert/andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /www/server/panel/vhost/cert/andiamo-backend.elenmorcreative.com/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers off;

    # General Settings
    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN" always;
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
    index index.php;
    charset utf-8;

    # Laravel Rewrite Rule
    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.php?$query_string;
    }

    # Standard PHP Block
    location ~ \.php$ {
        try_files $uri =404;
        fastcgi_split_path_info ^(.+\.php)(/.+)$;
        fastcgi_pass unix:/tmp/php-cgi-83.sock; # My PHP version is 8.3
        fastcgi_index index.php;
        include fastcgi_params;
        fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME $document_root$fastcgi_script_name;
        fastcgi_param PATH_INFO $fastcgi_path_info;
    }

    # Block sensitive files
    location ~ /\.(?!well-known).* {
        deny all;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>My Question:</strong>
Given this evidence, what specific setting in aapanel or Nginx could be preventing PHP sessions from persisting across cross-domain requests, even when <code>withCredentials</code> is true and cookies are being sent? Is there a security module, a specific <code>fastcgi_param</code>, or a proxy setting in aapanel that I am missing?</p>
<p>Thank you for your help.</p>
